# Supply-chain delay: only install package versions that have been public for
# at least this many days, so a compromised release has a window to be caught
# and unpublished before it can reach a build here.
#
# The key is `min-release-age` (kebab-case) and the value is a bare NUMBER OF
# DAYS. Source of truth is npm's own config schema — `hint: '<days>'`,
# `type: [null, Number]` in @npmcli/config's `lib/definitions/definitions.js`
# (`npm config ls -l` prints values and their origin, NOT the schema, so it
# won't show you the units). Quickest check without reading npm's source: set
# a bad value and run any npm command — npm names the expected type.
# This file previously read `minReleaseAge=7d`, which was wrong twice over and
# silently did nothing: npm reported `Unknown project config "minReleaseAge"`
# on every command, and `npm config get min-release-age` returned `null`. The
# `7d` value is rejected too — `invalid config ... Must be one of: null,
# numeric value`. Verified against npm 11.17.
#
# NOTE this does not reach `container/Dockerfile`'s `RUN npm install -g`: the
# docker build context is `container/`, so this file is never copied in. Those
# packages are pinned literally + watched by Renovate instead (#796).
min-release-age=7
